docs/nit-269-handoff.md:51:- A buyer describes the agent they are building in the Neatlogs-styled entry page; one server-side agent interprets the request, proposes a `ScenarioProposal`, deterministic code validates it, publishes the validated dataset (with the story and the run state) in one SQLite transaction, reads it back independently, and the reply cites only spans that exist in the read-back trace. The demo then shows the personalised trace in the list, detail, graph and timeline views.
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:55:- The provider label is server-owned: `GET /api/execution` (and `execution` on session reads and turn responses) reports mode, provider, model and effort, and the client paints it on every page, new tab and `/entry`. Mock shows "Mock-backed"; live shows "Live model · <model>"; if the endpoint fails the UI says "Provider status unavailable" and never a mock- or live-looking label. Enter sends the message (Shift+Enter inserts a newline). HTTP 5xx and network failures are reported as unconfirmed/not sent; "Nothing was changed" appears only for 4xx rejections.
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:69:3. **Settle** (transaction 3): the buyer turn's final status, Tailor's turns and the stored response are appended and the run becomes `settled`.
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:89:Each provider call is owned by the provider: **60 s** per call with an `AbortController` abort and the SDK's own `timeout` set to the same value; SDK `maxRetries` is **0** (Tailor owns retries). The core's per-call backstop is 65 s, and a **90 s turn deadline** bounds all calls in a turn; no call starts with less than 5 s left. A timeout is a `ProviderError('timeout')` marked non-retryable, so timeouts are **not retried within a turn**. A timed-out call settles at its worst-case reservation as unreconciled (see Spend guard).
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:117:Returns `telemetry.diagnostics()`: `mode`, `deliveryState` (`disabled`, `captured`, `unknown` = nothing confirmed yet, `healthy`, `degraded` = delivering again but spans were lost earlier, `failing` = latest export failed and nothing has succeeded since), `exportFailures`, `droppedSteps`, `droppedAttributes`, `droppedSpans`/`droppedExports`, `sdkFailures`, `redactedValues`, `syntheticPayloads`, `endpoint` (origin only), `queued` (null = not inspectable), `exportedSpans` (Tailor spans ended; excludes the SDK's per-trace `neatlogs.trace.complete` marker, which is counted separately as `markerSpans` — the SDK's own failure counter still includes markers), `lastExportError` (class, code, HTTP status; never bodies), `lastSuccessfulExportAt`, and `recentTurns` (last 20: `turnId` → `correlationId` → `traceId` → `turnRef`, status, outcome). `turnId` is the API's `turnId` and `turnRef` is the hashed `tailor.turn_id` that appears on spans, so an operator can go from a turn response to the hosted trace. `flush()` returns `{ok, exported, failed, state, timedOut}` (read `.ok`; it is false while an earlier failure is unresolved). `shutdown()` is bounded (5 s) and writes one summary line in `neatlogs` mode. **SIGKILL or any exit that bypasses `shutdown()` loses the current batch.**
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:123:- **Hosted:** `TAILOR_TELEMETRY=neatlogs TAILOR_PROVIDER=mock node --env-file-if-exists=.env.local scripts/verify-hosted.ts --probe-trace=<Doctor probe trace id>`. It refuses unless the telemetry and provider modes are right, the endpoint is the hosted origin, and a key is present. **Identity gate:** it reads the Doctor probe trace back with the same key and refuses (exit 4, nothing sent) unless the trace's `projectId` is the Tailor project `18c4b4b6-0b1e-4d41-9405-7960fc2d0aa8`, and refuses outright for the learning sandbox `a35a7334-258e-441c-b2e1-b8198a263f21`. (The probe's own JSON does not name a project, so the probe trace's read-back project is the identification.) Then it boots the real server on an ephemeral 127.0.0.1 port with a temporary SQLite, runs four SYNTHETIC-labelled turns (clarify, build, explain, one refused), flushes (`flush().ok` must be true), requires one trace id per turn from `/api/dev/telemetry`, and polls the hosted read-back (up to 60 s) until every trace is found with `projectId` equal to the Tailor project; it reports status, span count and span names per trace. Exit 0 = PASS. (The span counts in the table below were compared with a local captured-mode replay by hand; the script does not assert counts.)
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:128:- **Before 2026-10-12** the Tailor project (`18c4b4b6-0b1e-4d41-9405-7960fc2d0aa8`) held exactly two traces (the synthetic `npm run smoke:trace` trace and the SDK Doctor probe, both 2026-10-10) and no `tailor.turn`; every earlier run (mock, Playwright, the live-model session) used `captured` mode, which disables export. The empty-looking project was expected, not an ingestion failure.
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:143:- `TAILOR_PROVIDER=mock` is the default and the only provider used by `npm test`, `npm run test:ui` and `npm run dev`. The mock (`src/clients/neatlogs/mock-provider.ts`, over the generic offline shell in `src/tailor/mock-provider.ts`) is heuristic: it recognises a bounded set of domains and failure shapes, so "unfamiliar wording" works to the extent the heuristics cover it. The UI labels this honestly ("Mock-backed" chip; "Mock-backed prototype — no live model yet"), now from the server's `GET /api/execution` on every page. A live run is the separate, approval-gated `npm run dev:live`, and never falls back to the mock.
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:167:| Output | structured JSON via `output_config.format` (untransformed `json_schema`), no tools / `tool_choice`. Calls use `client.messages.create` and Tailor parses the first text block itself, so usage is recorded before any parse error and `stop_reason` is read first (the earlier `messages.parse` path threw before `stop_reason`, leaving truncated/refused calls unrecorded) | Structured outputs docs; real-SDK tests with scripted `fetch` (`test/provider-sdk-wire.test.ts`) |
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:170:Per turn: one planning call (interpret, choose a supported action, propose a scenario or ask one three-choice clarification) and, after a successful save and read-back, one explanation call that receives only the read-back evidence. Knowledge retrieval, permissions, schema/timing/relationship validation, persistence, version checks and read-back stay in deterministic code; model output is validated structurally and grounded before use. Refusals (`stop_reason: refusal`), truncation (`max_tokens`), malformed output, context-window overflow, 401/403/402, 429, 529, timeouts and network errors are mapped to a `ProviderErrorKind`, recorded in telemetry (`provider_error_kind`, `retryable`, `request_id`) and surfaced to the buyer as a generic "nothing changed" reply. Refuse and unsupported prose passes the same claim filters as answers, so invented prices, plans and capabilities are dropped. Timeouts: see "Timeouts" above (60 s per call, 90 s turn deadline, no in-turn retry of timeouts).
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:177:- **Rates:** per model id (`MODEL_RATES`); Sonnet 5.5 is $2 input / $10 output / $0.10 cache read / $2.50 cache write (5-minute) / $4 cache write (1-hour) per MTok. A model without a rate entry is refused at startup. Cache writes are priced at the 1-hour rate because usage does not report the TTL split (conservative); caching is not enabled.
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:191:1. Open `http://127.0.0.1:4321/entry`; the panel shows "Powered by Tailor · synthetic data" and the mode label from the server: "Live model · claude-sonnet-5-5" for a live run (no "Mock-backed" label), or "Mock-backed" for `npm run dev`. If it says "Provider status unavailable", stop and check `GET /api/execution`. Type the message and press **Enter** to send (Shift+Enter inserts a newline).
docs/nit-269-handoff.md:203:2. **`TAILOR_TELEMETRY=neatlogs` for a live run is a separate approval** (external ingestion of live-model telemetry). The 2026-10-12 hosted verification used the mock provider only; it does not cover live turns. Confirm the Tailor project and region and keep the key in the process environment.
